WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.943
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.080
- Hi everybody, today I'm gonna talk

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.530
about another topic regarding entomology,

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:15.100
specifically the importance of migration

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.193
to butterfly and moth populations.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:21.260
Migration is a very important
part of the life cycle

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.060
of lots of butterflies and moths.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:26.810
Although it's not, it's not
very common to be honest,

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.810
there's a lot of species,
the majority of butterflies

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:30.390
and moths species are fairly sedentary,

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.500
meaning they have very focused ranges,

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.110
and they don't migrate much.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.320
But there are notable exceptions,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.620
and the way humans behave, and
the way humans are stewards

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.510
of their environment is very important,

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.730
to be able to map, and track

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:48.110
and also protect the migration routes,

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:51.060
of these particular butterflies and moths.

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:55.060
So probably the most important migrator,

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.230
that I'm sure everybody has heard about,

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.663
is the monarch butterfly.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:02.470
The monarch butterfly is very well known,

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:05.400
and it is a very strong migrator,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.370
at least most populations are.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:11.730
And this species as an
adult, ranges from Canada,

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:12.990
all the way to Mexico,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.060
and individuals over winter
in the mountains of Mexico

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.360
in the fir forest at high elevation.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.070
And they have spectacular
breeding aggregations

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.223
in the mountains of Mexico.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.380
So a lot of people have a little,

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.610
have some misconceptions about migration,

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.230
like what do they do?

00:01:30.230 --> 00:01:32.403
Do they all do make the flight at once?

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.150
But they don't necessarily
make the flight at once.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.620
So this is the example
of the queen butterfly,

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.550
which is the same genus,
same family as the monarch,

00:01:43.550 --> 00:01:44.630
and the queen butterfly

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:47.410
doesn't migrate very much, very rarely.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.200
Most of these live in the Southeast US,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.250
down into the Caribbean and
also central and South America,

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:55.130
and they have a very sedentary existence,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.100
they don't migrate much.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.820
The monarch starts off
in the spring in Mexico,

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.830
and individuals fly up through,
basically through Mexico,

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.620
into the Southern United States,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.350
through Texas and
California and reproduce.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:12.360
So an individual monarch doesn't go

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.920
from Mexico to say New
York or Connecticut.

00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:17.310
It will have several generations

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:18.960
that it takes to get up,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:23.750
from Mexico to like
basically the Northeast,

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:25.500
because the butterflies
don't live that long,

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.410
there's a lot of challenges along the way.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.140
So the butterflies that you're seeing now,

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.300
like in the fall in Connecticut
are going to be several gen,

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.320
are going to be like one
or two generations separate

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.763
from the moths that left
Mexico in the spring.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:39.450
And then, basically when there's

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.900
to get these subtle cues
from the environment,

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:45.140
they turn around and
decide to return to Mexico,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.570
which is a mystery that
science is still trying

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:48.930
to figure out how they do this.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.870
But it's like when it gets cooler,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.450
the monarchs turn around and
start flooding back South,

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.040
usually along the coast.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.470
And a lot of people will see a lot

00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.910
of monarchs around this time of year,

00:02:58.910 --> 00:03:01.273
because monarchs are extremely,

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.340
like they're very sensitive to cold,

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.240
so they cannot survive winter
temperatures in the Northeast,

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:09.390
so they have to immediately return South.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.210
So over multiple generations again,

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.610
these, a lot of adult
monarchs will actually move

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.810
from, say the Northeast
down to the mid Atlantic,

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.390
or the Southeast, and then
have another generation.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:26.420
And then those next generation
monarchs will continue

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.370
to Mexico or the Caribbean.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:31.440
So again, a single monarch,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.730
usually doesn't make the whole trip.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.770
There might be a few that
are strong enough to do it,

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:35.980
but most of them take

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.030
a couple of generations to make the trip.

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.620
And so given basically

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.480
how we can affect migrators
becomes very apparent

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.160
when I talk about these next two species,

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.890
which are called, this is
the common painted lady,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:53.900
which if you have children,

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:56.870
a lot of people sell
rearing kits for these,

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.070
where children can rear,

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.690
and rear butterflies from eggs to adult,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:01.990
and they're called painted ladies,

00:04:01.990 --> 00:04:04.310
they're very common species.

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.480
And this butterfly here is
called the common snout,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.180
they're called snouts,
'cause they have a little,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.730
like their palps are
kind of pointed into a,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.300
it looks like a nose, this
is another migratory species.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.320
And sometimes in the Southwest,
these things are so abundant

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.360
that there could be literally
swarms of billions of them,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:22.690
flying through the Southwest,

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:24.690
and people have drove through them,

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:28.340
and just darkens the skies,
there's so many of these.

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.240
So when it comes to migrators,
the common denominator

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.290
in a lot of these is the Southwest.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.490
So in the Southwestern United States,

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:42.500
you have a lot of concentration
of butterfly diversity.

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.970
And a lot of our species that
we get up into the Northeast,

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.960
of all different types,
pass through that area.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.210
And so a big challenge for
going forward for humanity,

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:55.170
is to actually protect the areas,

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.230
not only in Mexico, in Latin America,

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:00.200
where these species is over winter,

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:02.100
but also protecting habitat,

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.600
that these species use as corridors,

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:08.320
to basically fuel up for their journey.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.070
So old farmland, old fields,
abandoned areas with flowers,

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:18.070
are very important for
basically preserving the habitat

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.120
of these butterflies and moths.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:23.030
And a lot of these
butterflies actually feed on,

00:05:23.030 --> 00:05:24.650
like these feed on milkweeds.

00:05:24.650 --> 00:05:26.470
So if you have milkweeds in your yard,

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:28.330
don't, don't chop them down, keep them,

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:30.310
because these butterflies will use them,

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.800
both for fueling the next generation,

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.220
and also food for nectaring.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:40.100
So, and another issue is
associated with the border wall,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:42.900
that's unfortunately being proposed

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.650
to going up along the Texas

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:47.940
and New Mexico and California border.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.170
And this wall is having the adverse effect

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:54.610
of potentially destroying
lots of key habitat,

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.530
for butterflies and moths
right along the Texas border.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:00.980
And there's a very strong center

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:02.800
of diversity in Southern Texas,

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:05.880
it's one of the most species rich areas,

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:09.560
of all the areas of the
United States for butterflies,

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.460
and actually the butterfly,

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.510
one of the major butterfly
gardens in Southern Texas

00:06:14.510 --> 00:06:17.450
is actually partially
destroyed by construction crews

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:18.760
that are building the wall.

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:21.960
So even by doing things that seem

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.960
to be based more focused on humans,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.460
they have adverse impacts
on the environment,

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.220
by causing that
destruction to the habitat,

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:32.930
both to the plants that
the insects depend on,

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:37.080
and also killing potential
caterpillars eggs and pupa

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.427
of adult butterflies and moths as well.

00:06:39.427 --> 00:06:42.010
(upbeat music)

